│ ▾ wiki                               ││   The Science of a Good Loaf                                                                                         ││                                      │
│   ▾ comparisons                      ││                                                                                                                      ││ Sources  0                           │
│       commercial-yeast-vs-sourdough  ││   Great bread is a small set of processes happening in the same bowl: starches hydrating, proteins                   ││                                      │
│       kneading-vs-folding            ││   linking into gluten, yeasts producing gas, and heat finally setting the crumb. Understanding each                  ││                                      │
│   ▾ concepts                         ││   process in isolation makes most recipes legible, because nearly every instruction in a bread                       ││                                      │
│       autolyse                       ││   recipe exists to favour one of these processes over another.                                                       ││                                      │
│       windowpane-test                ││                                                                                                                      ││                                      │
│   ▾ entities                         ││   Flour, water and time                                                                                              ││                                      │
│       banneton                       ││                                                                                                                      ││                                      │
│       dutch-oven                     ││   Wheat flour carries two storage proteins, glutenin and gliadin. When water is added and the flour                  ││                                      │
│                                      ││   rests, they unwind and begin to bond into an elastic network. Given time and gentle handling that                  ││                                      │
│                                      ││   network develops on its own, which is why a long rest can replace much of the hard kneading a                      ││                                      │
│                                      ││   recipe asks for.                                                                                                   ││                                      │
│                                      ││                                                                                                                      ││                                      │
│                                      ││   Enzymes in the flour, mainly amylase, slowly cut starches into sugars. Those sugars feed the yeast                 ││                                      │
│                                      ││   and, later, sweeten the crust through browning. A dough that rests longer tastes faintly sweeter                   ││                                      │
│                                      ││   and browns deeper for exactly this reason.                                                                         ││                                      │
│                                      ││                                                                                                                      ││                                      │
│                                      ││   Gas, structure and heat                                                                                            ││                                      │
│                                      ││                                                                                                                      ││                                      │
│                                      ││   Fermentation produces carbon dioxide that collects inside the gluten network. In the oven the gas                  ││                                      │
│                                      ││   expands, the loaf springs upward, and heat sets the crumb, turning a soft paste into a sliceable                   ││                                      │
│                                      ││   structure. The baker's job is less about forcing any of this and more about timing it.                             ││                                      │
│                                      ││                                                                                                                      ││                                      │
│                                      ││   Ratios                                                                                                             ││                                      │
│                                      ││                                                                                                                      ││                                      │
│                                      ││   Professional bakers describe every formula as a set of percentages of the flour weight. Writing a                  ││                                      │
│                                      ││   recipe that way makes hydration explicit and lets a baker scale the formula to any amount of flour                 ││                                      │
│                                      ││   without arithmetic surprises.                                                                                      ││                                      │
